PLACES=[
N('bartow-carnegie-library-site','Site of the Bartow Carnegie Library','Bartow','fl','civic','gone',27.89420,-81.84109,'area','view','walk','','1911–1998',
  "Bartow won an $8,000 Carnegie grant on March 18, 1911, says Wikipedia's list; the St. Petersburg library's Register form also counts Bartow's 1911 grant among the ten that built public libraries in Florida. The library stood at 310 East Stanford Street and was pulled down in 1998. No history of the building itself was found.",
  'Nothing of the library stands.',
  '','310 East Stanford Street. Nothing is left to see.',
  [LK,ML],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('bradenton-carnegie-library-fl','Bradenton Carnegie Library (Manatee County Historical Records Library)','Bradenton','fl','civic','standing',27.49641,-82.57551,'exact','open','walk','','1918–',
  "Bradenton first asked for a Carnegie library around 1906 and was turned down; after more talks it won $10,000 (granted February 3, 1917, says Wikipedia's list). T. W. Hullinger and Son built it in 1918 to one of Carnegie's standard plans, in the Classical Revival style, two storeys high. It was the city's main library for 60 years and the base of the county's first bookmobile in 1956, the year an addition was built. After the Central Library opened in 1978 the county clerk took it over, and on June 1, 1979 it opened as the Historical Records Library, the county government's archive. On the National Register since 1987.",
  'Doric columns, an original lamp at the west entrance, and a marble panel lettered in relief in the front pediment. The 1956 addition hides the old front on 14th Street West and keeps its original shelving and curved desk. Inside are records back to 1856, marriage licences, maps and old postcards.',
  '','1405 4th Avenue West, though the building faces 14th Street West. Run by the '+MC+'; hours from the clerk\'s page.',
  [['Manatee County Clerk: Historical Records Library','https://www.manateeclerk.com/departments/historical-resources/manatee-county-historical-records-library/'],W('Bradenton Carnegie Library'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/87000616'],['National Register nomination','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/0ebe790e-7cf7-4e3e-8e61-be5ff66366e2'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='Mon–Fri 8:30–4:30'),
N('clearwater-carnegie-library-site','Site of the Clearwater Carnegie Library','Clearwater','fl','civic','gone',27.96750,-82.80140,'site','view','walk','','1916–about 2000',
  "Carnegie gave Clearwater $10,000 (granted March 16, 1915, says Wikipedia's list), and a special tax paid for the site at Osceola Avenue. F. J. Kennard of Tampa drew the library and G. A. Miller built it; it opened in September 1916 with over 2,700 books. It was enlarged in 1939, repaired in 1942, extended again in 1961 against advice to pull it down, and renovated after 1978. Voters approved a new Main Library in July 2000; the old building was pulled down about then, and the new library, by Robert A. M. Stern, opened on its ground on May 1, 2004.",
  'Nothing of the Carnegie building stands; the Main Library is on its ground.',
  '','100 North Osceola Avenue, where the Main Library now stands, downtown. Nothing of the Carnegie building is left.',
  [['St Pete Catalyst: Clearwater celebrates library\'s Carnegie connection (2026)','https://stpetecatalyst.com/clearwater-celebrates-librarys-carnegie-connection/'],W('Clearwater Public Library System'),LK],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('gainesville-carnegie-library-site-fl','Site of the Gainesville Carnegie Library','Gainesville','fl','civic','gone',29.65188,-82.32152,'area','view','walk','','1918–1954',
  "The city signed a contract with the Carnegie Corporation for $10,000 (granted March 31, 1916, says Wikipedia's list). Changes of architect slowed the work, and the library opened on February 25 at 419 East University Avenue, beside Sweetwater Branch; the county library's history calls it the 1918 building. When it grew crowded the architect Myrl Hanes advised against remodelling it, and a new library was built on the same ground. Wikipedia's list says the Carnegie building was pulled down in 1954.",
  'Nothing of the library stands.',
  '','419 East University Avenue, by Sweetwater Branch, downtown. Nothing of the Carnegie building is left.',
  [['Alachua County Library District: history','https://aclib.us/history'],LK],brick='?'),
N('jacksonville-carnegie-library-fl','Old Jacksonville Free Public Library (Bedell Building)','Jacksonville','fl','civic','standing',30.32761,-81.65591,'exact','view','walk','','1903–1905',
  "Rebuilding after the Great Fire of 1901, Jacksonville was offered Carnegie money in 1902 on condition that it found a site and spent at least $5,000 a year on the library (Wikipedia's library article says $50,000; its list says $55,000, granted February 13, 1902). Voters accepted, 640 to 627. Henry John Klutho drew it; ground was broken on October 3, 1903 at the north-east corner of Adams and Ocean Streets, and it opened on June 1, 1905, the first tax-supported library in Florida. Since 1984 a law firm has owned and used it, and it is known as the Bedell Building. On the National Register since 1987.",
  'A two-storey Classical Revival building of limestone, with four Ionic columns across the front; wood was used only in the floors, doors and window sashes.',
  '','101 East Adams Street, at North Ocean Street, downtown. Privately owned (law offices): look from the street.',
  [W('Jacksonville Public Library'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/86003679'],LK],
  brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('ocala-carnegie-library-site','Site of the Ocala Carnegie Library','Ocala','fl','civic','gone',29.1871,-82.1348,'area','view','walk','','1916–1968',
  "Ocala's library began as a subscription collection in 1886, and the Women's Library Association took it on in 1890. Carnegie granted $10,000 on February 21, 1907, says Wikipedia's list, but the library on Ocklawaha Avenue did not open until 8 a.m. on September 11, 1916, still only partly furnished and with no ceremony. It was in the Spanish Mission style with a red clay tile roof: stacks, two alcoves and a fireplace on the main floor, and a meeting hall in the basement. Louise Gamsby was its librarian from 1916 to 1951. It was pulled down in 1968 for a larger library.",
  'Nothing of the library stands.',
  '','On Ocklawaha Avenue (206, says Wikipedia\'s list). Where on the street it stood was not settled. Nothing is left to see.',
  [W('Ocala Carnegie Library'),LK],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('palmetto-carnegie-library-fl','Palmetto Carnegie Library (Palmetto Historical Park)','Palmetto','fl','civic','standing',27.51595,-82.57584,'exact','open','walk','','1914–',
  "Built in 1914 with a $10,000 Carnegie grant (January 14, 1914, says Wikipedia's list) on land the city gave; getting it built was a big achievement of the town's women's club, founded in 1900. Because Carnegie wanted a basement, the main floor is upstairs, reached by steps to the front door, and the ground floor counts as the basement. It is now the centre of the Palmetto Historical Park, run jointly by the city, the county clerk, the county and the Palmetto Historical Commission, whose offices are inside, with the Alice V. Myers Archives Center of local family histories, photographs and records. Palmetto's 1880 post office, saved from demolition by the commission from 1982, was moved behind it. In the Palmetto Historic District, on the National Register since 1986.",
  'The park\'s page says the library can be visited in open hours without a tour; a free guided tour is needed for the park\'s other buildings: the 1880 post office, a cottage museum, a schoolhouse, a military museum and a replica chapel.',
  '','515 10th Avenue West. Street parking, and a lot behind the park to the west, off 6th Street West. The park\'s page says school programmes run on weekdays from 10 to 1, September to May, and asks visitors to call ahead.',
  [['Palmetto Historical Park','https://palmettohistoricalpark.com/'],[MC+': Palmetto Historical Park','https://manateeclerk.com/departments/historical-resources/palmetto-historical-park/'],W('Palmetto Historical Park'),W('Palmetto Historic District'),LK],
  brick='?',hours='Tue–Fri and the 1st and 3rd Saturday, 10–12 and 1–4'),
N('mirror-lake-library-st-petersburg','Mirror Lake Community Library (St. Petersburg Carnegie Library)','St. Petersburg','fl','civic','standing',27.77477,-82.64026,'exact','open','walk','','1914–1915',
  "After a five-year campaign St. Petersburg won $17,500 from Carnegie (granted July 9, 1913, says Wikipedia's list; the Register form says the city commission approved it on July 27, 1914). Henry D. Whitfield drew a Beaux-Arts library to Carnegie's standard plan, and it was built on city land filled in on the shore of the old reservoir, now Mirror Lake; Walter C. Henry began work in October 1914 and finished on September 11, 1915. Black residents were refused until 1944, and then let into the basement only. It was the city's only library until the main library opened in 1964. A 1951 wing was taken down in a restoration: the old building reopened in 1994 and a new wing in 1997. On the National Register since 1986, and still a branch library.",
  'Buff brick, now covered in stucco, with cast concrete ornament, a parapet round the gabled roof, big Palladian windows at each end and PVBLIC LIBRARY over the east door. Its front lines up with the middle of 3rd Avenue North. The Register form found the main-floor rooms remarkably intact.',
  '','280 5th Street North, on the east shore of Mirror Lake, downtown. A branch of the city library; its hours were not checked.',
  [W('Mirror Lake Library'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/86001259'],ML,LK],
  brick='no',hours='?',conf='mixed'),
N('tampa-free-public-library-fl','Old Tampa Free Public Library','Tampa','fl','civic','standing',27.96066,-82.46057,'exact','view','walk','','1915–1968',
  "Louise Frances Dodge of the Tampa Tribune began the push for Carnegie money in 1905. Wikipedia's list dates the grant December 30, 1901; the building's article says $25,000 was granted in 1912 and raised to $50,000 in 1913. Fred J. James of Tampa drew it, and it was finished on June 30, 1915, but stood shut until April 27, 1917, while the city council argued over paying to run it. It was Tampa's main library until 1968, then a school for gifted children until 1986. The city sold it for $1 in 1987, renovated it in 1999 for city offices, and since 2016 it has held the county library system's offices. On the National Register since 1991.",
  'Yellow and brown brick on a rusticated granite basement, with a clay tile roof; a flared granite stair leads to paired Tuscan columns and tall brick pilasters at the entrance.',
  '','102 East 7th Avenue, in Tampa Heights. Offices: look from the street.',
  [W('Tampa Free Library'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/91000618'],['National Register nomination','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/046481d1-dfeb-49d3-a436-12d1506a1564'],LK],
  brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('west-tampa-carnegie-library','West Tampa Free Public Library (West Tampa Branch Library)','Tampa','fl','civic','standing',27.95776,-82.48342,'exact','open','walk','','1913–',
  "West Tampa, then a city of its own built on cigar making, voted 352 to 1 to take a $17,500 Carnegie grant (January 2, 1913, says Wikipedia's list) and a tax to run it; the cigar factory owner Angel Cuesta gave the land. The library says it was built in 1913; Wikipedia says it opened in 1914, the first public library in Hillsborough County, with a Spanish-language reading section for the cigar workers. It is in the West Tampa historic district. A 5,000-square-foot addition was built in 2002 and 2003, and the old building's refurbishment ended in April 2004. Still a public library.",
  'A brick Neoclassical Revival building with stained glass, and a triptych by Ferdie Pacheco of cigar workers in the foyer.',
  '','2312 West Union Street. A branch of the county library; its page gave no hours.',
  [['Tampa-Hillsborough County Public Library: West Tampa Branch','http://www.hcplc.org/hcplc/locations/wtp/'],W('West Tampa Free Public Library'),LK],
  brick='?',hours='?',conf='mixed'),
N('fessenden-academy-carnegie-hall-site','Site of Carnegie Hall, Fessenden Academy','Ocala (Martin)','fl','civic','gone',29.28077,-82.19251,'area','view','drive','','1909–',
  "Fessenden Academy began in 1868, founded by freedmen led by Thomas B. Ward, and grew with help from Ferdinand S. Fessenden from 1890 and the American Missionary Association. Its principal, Joseph L. Wiley, won a $6,500 Carnegie grant (March 15, 1905, says Wikipedia's list), and with $1,500 more in gifts the main building went up in 1909, holding the library, rooms for girl boarders, a dining hall and classrooms. The county school board took over the school in the early 1950s (1951 or 1952; the sources differ). The Register form says the buildings put up after 1900 were replaced in 1931, yet lists a Carnegie Hall, the girls' dormitory, among those standing in 1952; it and the other old dormitories were later pulled down or burned.",
  'Nothing of Carnegie Hall stands. Three later buildings of local stone survive (Chapman Hall and the old Union School of 1931, and the Walthall building of 1946), with Fessenden\'s burial marker in front of Chapman Hall. They form the Old Fessenden Academy Historic District, on the National Register since 1994.',
  '','4200 NW 89th Place, about nine miles north-west of downtown Ocala. A working public elementary school: look from the road.',
  [W('Fessenden Elementary School'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/94001141'],['National Register nomination','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/852784d0-dadd-403f-be40-29a69a6c5fe6'],LK],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('famu-carnegie-library-tallahassee','Carnegie Library, Florida A&M University (Meek-Eaton Black Archives)','Tallahassee','fl','inst','standing',30.4275,-84.28639,'site','open','walk','','1905–1908',
  "Carnegie gave $10,000 (granted July 18, 1905, says Wikipedia's list); Wikipedia says the city of Tallahassee had turned a Carnegie library down because it would have had to serve Black readers. In December 1905 a fire destroyed the college's main building, Duval Hall, with its library; Wikipedia says the new library was built on its site. Drawn by William Augustus Edwards, it was dedicated in February 1908 at the State Normal and Industrial College for Colored Students, now FAMU: the only Carnegie library on a Black land-grant college campus. The Black Archives, founded by the legislature in 1971, moved in in 1976, and later spread to the Union Bank building and a new building of 2006. Listed on the National Register in 1978 (the record's date; Wikipedia's text says 1996).",
  'A square two-storey building of white brick with a hipped roof and a pedimented portico of fluted Ionic columns on square plinths; the windows sit in brick panels with jack arches and keystones.',
  '','On the FAMU campus. Visitors need a parking decal from the university\'s Parking Services. The Black Archives\' page gives its hours, asks visitors to call ahead on Saturdays, and charges no admission; it does not say which of its buildings is open.',
  [['FAMU: Meek-Eaton Black Archives, plan your visit','https://famu.edu/academics/libraries/meek-eaton-black-archives-research-center-and-museum/meba-plan-your-visit.php'],W('Carnegie Library at FAMU'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/78000949'],['National Register nomination','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/6171fb97-1b1c-4dff-a2cd-ff243baeae7c'],LK],
  brick='no',hours='Tue–Fri 10–4; Sat 11–3, call ahead',conf='mixed'),
N('stetson-sampson-hall-deland','Sampson Hall (old Carnegie library), Stetson University','DeLand','fl','inst','standing',29.03444,-81.30261,'exact','view','walk','','1906–1908',
  "Carnegie gave John B. Stetson University $40,000 on March 12, 1906, the largest of his gifts to a Florida college, matched by Elizabeth Stetson. Henry John Klutho drew the Neoclassical library, which opened in 1908 and was named for C. T. Sampson, a trustee. It was the library for 56 years; in 1964 students and staff carried more than 100,000 books by hand to the new duPont-Ball Library. It now holds classrooms, offices and art studios. In the Stetson University Campus Historic District, on the National Register since 1991.",
  'EDUCATION IS POWER across the front in Roman letters, and the names of writers such as Chaucer, Tennyson and Longfellow round the building, on the palm court in the middle of the campus.',
  '','On the Stetson University campus, off East Minnesota Avenue. A university building: look from the campus walks.',
  [LK,W('Stetson University Campus Historic District')],brick='?'),
N('rollins-carnegie-hall-winter-park','Carnegie Hall (old library), Rollins College','Winter Park','fl','inst','standing',28.59208,-81.34933,'exact','view','walk','','1908–1909',
  "Rollins' president William Fremont Blackman asked Carnegie for a library in 1904. A letter of June 22, 1905 offered $20,000 if the college raised as much for upkeep; Blackman feared it could not, but the trustees found the money. Carnegie Hall was built in 1908 and 1909 for about $19,000 and opened on February 18, 1909, the college's first library building and also its post office. It was the library until the Mills Memorial Library opened in 1951, and has since held a bookstore, the admissions office and other offices.",
  '',
  '','On Holt Avenue, on the Rollins College campus. A college building: look from the campus walks.',
  [['University of North Florida: Andrew Carnegie Hall, Rollins College','https://digitalcommons.unf.edu/historical_architecture_main/5937'],LK],brick='?'),
]
